.TH ATLAS 1 "October 2026" "ATLAS 1.6.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
atlas \- scoped Linux subdomain discovery and DNS evidence mapping
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B atlas menu
.br
.B atlas doctor
.br
.B atlas scan -d DOMAIN
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Combines certificate transparency, historical URL hosts and optional passive
executables. Active mode generates bounded DNS guesses, validates records,
marks possible wildcard answers, and optionally collects HTTP metadata.
Results are evidence, not a completeness or vulnerability guarantee.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B menu
Persistent numbered terminal dashboard with colors and optional figlet banner.
.TP
.B doctor
Display optional tool locations and Python DNS dependency availability.
.TP
.B scan
Enumerate within one root hostname; URLs and IP addresses are rejected.
.SH SCAN OPTIONS
.TP
.B -d, --domain DOMAIN
Required root domain, including IDNA-compatible international names.
.TP
.B --mode passive|active
Passive is the default. Active enables DNS discovery and validation.
.TP
.B --authorized
Required acknowledgment for active checks within your authorized scope.
.TP
.B --sources LIST
Comma-separated crtsh,certspotter,hackertarget,wayback,subfinder,assetfinder,findomain. All default on.
.TP
.B --import FILE
Import evidence; repeat for multiple files. Extract and scope-filter hostnames.
.TP
.B --offline
Skip passive network providers. Active mode still performs DNS/HTTP checks.
.TP
.B --exclude DOMAIN
Exclude a hostname and descendants. Repeatable.
.TP
.B -o, --output DIRECTORY
Parent directory for a new timestamped run. Default results.
.TP
.B -w, --wordlist FILE
DNS labels/prefixes, one per line; active only. Default bundled starter list.
.TP
.B --rate NUMBER
Positive built-in DNS query rate per second; default 10. Passed to external
tools where supported. Not a universal provider rate limit.
.TP
.B --timeout SECONDS
Positive per-provider request/DNS lifetime, default 10.
.TP
.B --tool-timeout SECONDS
External command timeout, default 600.
.TP
.B --archive-limit NUMBER
Maximum Wayback CDX records, default 10000; capped coverage.
.TP
.B --max-candidates NUMBER
Maximum generated guesses, default 10000. Does not cap passive evidence.
.TP
.B --recursive-depth 0|1|2|3
Expand wordlist under known names at or below this relative depth.
One expansion pass; default 0 means root wordlist only.
.TP
.B --wildcard-tests NUMBER
Random sibling samples per parent, default 3. Heuristic classification.
.TP
.B --permute
Use installed alterx for bounded permutations and enrichment; active only.
.TP
.B --engine builtin|puredns
DNS backend. PureDNS needs massdns and both resolver files. Builtin default.
.TP
.B --resolvers FILE
DNS resolver IPs, one per line. Builtin defaults to system DNS.
.TP
.B --trusted-resolvers FILE
Trusted validation resolver list, required for puredns.
.TP
.B --http
Use recognized ProjectDiscovery httpx to inventory resolved non-suspect hosts.
No redirect following or extracted-domain probing enabled. Active only.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Help for the current command. Use atlas scan --help for scan flags.
.TP
.B --version
Print version (top-level option).
.SH OUTPUT
subdomains.txt: discovered names excluding apex.
.br
resolved.txt: names currently resolving without detected wildcard overlap.
.br
assets.jsonl and assets.csv: names, sources, states, DNS records.
.br
summary.json: source outcomes, counts, timestamps and settings.
.br
wildcards.json: random sibling sample evidence.
.br
report.txt: plain-text inventory report.
.br
raw/: provider responses and external stdout/stderr logs.
.br
candidates.txt: active guesses, not proof of discovery.
.br
http.jsonl: optional HTTP metadata.
.SH STATES
passive-unverified, resolved, wildcard-suspect, unresolved, indeterminate.
Wildcard suspects are retained. DNS shared infrastructure/rotating answers can
produce false positives or negatives. Query errors are preserved where recorded.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B NO_COLOR
disables ANSI colors. PATH selects optional tools. Subfinder provider credentials
are configured using its own configuration files.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
atlas scan -d example.com
atlas scan -d example.com --offline --import names.txt
atlas scan -d example.com --mode active --authorized -w words.txt
atlas scan -d example.com --mode active --authorized --permute --http
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 successful (optional tools may be skipped); 1 provider/stage failure;
2 configuration or input failure; 130 interrupted with partial report.
.SH LIMITATIONS
No exhaustive-discovery guarantee. Private DNS, API quotas, archives, unknown
labels and exclusions constrain coverage. Negative generated answers are not
stored individually. Recursive expansion is bounded and uses initial seeds.
External CLI compatibility must be checked on Linux. No exploits or takeover
claims are performed. See README.md and RESEARCH.md for details and sources.
.SH SEE ALSO
subfinder, assetfinder, findomain, alterx, puredns, massdns, httpx

.SH TERMINAL REPORT
.B atlas report DIRECTORY
.br
Display a saved run overview, stage outcomes and asset inventory in the terminal.
No browser, GUI or HTML output is used.

.SH COLOR AND PAGINATION
.B atlas --color auto|always|never COMMAND
.br
Auto respects terminal detection and NO_COLOR. Always forces ANSI colors.
.br
.B atlas browse DIRECTORY
.br
Full numbered viewer with refresh, search and save.
.br
.B atlas report DIRECTORY --find TEXT --details
.br
Full numbered terminal viewer with optional search and evidence details.
Use --status to filter by the original machine-readable asset status.
.SH CERTIFICATE PAGINATION
.B --ct-pages NUMBER
limits Cert Spotter pages, default 5.
.br
.B --ct-after ID
continues from a saved issuance cursor. Import earlier evidence to combine runs.

.SH AVAILABILITY CHECKS
.B atlas check DIRECTORY --authorized
.br
Queue every discovered subdomain for background DNS and HTTP checks.
Options: --workers 1..32 (default 8), --rate NUMBER (default 10),
--timeout SECONDS (default 5), --dns-only, -o OUTPUT_PARENT.
Checks create a new run directory and preserve original discovery evidence.
.br
.B atlas scan -d DOMAIN --check-live --authorized
.br
Discover, then check every discovered name. --workers sets concurrency.
The explicit operation-start rate does not count internal DNS retries or
OS resolution performed by the HTTP stack.
.SH AVAILABILITY LABELS
UP means an HTTP response, including redirects and errors. NO RESPONSE means
neither HTTPS nor HTTP responded, not proven downtime. NO DNS means no usable
address returned. DNS ONLY means HTTP was not checked. CHECK ERROR is uncertainty.
CHECKING means pending. See --details for timestamps and evidence.
.SH SAVE AND FULL LIST
.B atlas export DIRECTORY -o FILE
.br
Save every subdomain as one hostname per line. --format csv|jsonl adds status
records. --availability UP selects only web-responsive names. --force permits
replacing an existing export.
.br
.B atlas report DIRECTORY
.br
Display the full list. --availability filters check results.
Interactive browse keys: r refresh, s search, l lookup by serial, w save, q exit. Full list shown immediately.
Menu 7 checks a saved run, 8 saves domains, 9 discovers and checks.
.SH CHECK FILES
availability.csv, subdomains.txt, up.txt, report.txt, assets.jsonl, summary.json.
Completed results are checkpointed; interrupted runs preserve pending rows.
No completeness or permanent-availability guarantee is provided.

.SH SERIAL NUMBERS
Every displayed subdomain has a serial number, starting at 1. The total count
appears below the full list. CSV exports include sr_no. Plain text domain
exports remain one hostname per line. Use terminal scrollback for large lists.

.SH DEFAULT STARTUP
.B atlas
.br
With no arguments, open the colorful Bash menu automatically. No menu command
or color flag is needed. Optional figlet produces a slant banner. A colored
text fallback works without figlet. NO_COLOR or ATLAS_COLOR=never disables
color. The Python entry point also opens a menu when started without arguments.
.SH SELECTED SUBDOMAIN LOOKUPS
After discovery, interactive terminals and menu scans offer l to look up a
serial number from the displayed results. No saved directory re-entry is needed.
Press Enter to finish. --lookup forces this prompt; --no-lookup suppresses it
for scripted scans. Non-interactive scans do not prompt by default.
With --check-live, selection uses the newly checked run and its displayed order.
.br
.B atlas lookup DIRECTORY
.br
Display full numbered results and choose a serial number, then WHOIS/RDAP,
IP/DNS/ASN, reverse IP, or all. Menu option 10 opens this workflow.
.br
.B atlas lookup DIRECTORY --serial NUMBER --kind whois|ip|reverse-ip|all
.br
Use the same saved directory and --find filter as the displayed report.
Serials start at 1 in each view and exclude the apex. Default kind is all.
WHOIS uses domain RDAP via IANA bootstrap and the registered parent from the
bundled Public Suffix List (private platform suffixes excluded). Subdomains
generally have no separate registration. Some ccTLDs lack RDAP.
--legacy-whois instead invokes the optional installed whois command.
.br
IP lookup queries A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, NS, TXT, CAA and SOA with TTLs, plus
PTR, regional registry RDAP, ASN DNS and HTTPS RIPEstat routing fallback.
Registration country is not device geolocation.
.br
Reverse IP includes PTR, shared-IP matches from saved A/AAAA evidence and
HackerTarget's partial IPv4 passive index. IPv6 external index is unsupported;
PTR/local correlation remains available. Non-public IPs skip enrichment.
--no-external disables only HackerTarget, not DNS/RDAP/routing services.
Outside-scope and excluded neighbours are labeled and never probed or
automatically added to discovery. Shared IP is not proof of common ownership.
.SS Limits and evidence
--timeout SECONDS: per-request timeout 1..30, default 8.
.br
--max-ips COUNT: IP enrichment limit 1..32, default 8. Omitted IPs are listed.
.br
--refresh: bypass successful response cache (one hour; IANA bootstrap 24 hours).
DNS queries are always fresh. No automatic provider retries or recursive
contact lookups. Provider quotas and errors remain visible.
.br
-o FILE: save JSON to a new file; existing files are never overwritten.
Default evidence path is DIRECTORY/lookups/, with cached provider data under
lookups/cache/. Raw and structured results include retrieval timestamps.
Partial lookup evidence is preserved; exit code 1 indicates incomplete results,
2 input/configuration errors. Reinstall requirements after upgrading.
